  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn the wake of cold frontal passage this morning northwesterly\nflow bringing in an abundant of dry air aloft along with \nsurface high pressure building in will provide quiet weather to\nthe area for the rest of today and possibly through midweek. A \nnice cool down period will commence as well as dew pints only \nclimbing to the 60s during the daytime and 50s during the \novernights which will promote dry and cool days through \nmidweek. During the daytime, highs will stay well below \nseasonable for this time of year and the region will mainly be \ndry for the most part. Daytime highs will return to around \nnormal after midweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next chance for shower or thunderstorm activity will be on \nThursday as another broad low pressure system traversing across\nthe Great Lakes bringing unsettled weather to the region. This \nfeature will drop in a cold frontal boundary through the area \nand will slide through into Friday. It is a little too far out \nto determine the severe threat, however forecast soundings \nindicate weak instability and shear so the severe parameters \nare not impressive as of now. \n\nOne thing there is an abundant of is moisture and long skinny\nCAPE along with high DCAPE values and PWATs, therefore the \nenvironment might support the threat of flash flooding although\nafter the aforementioned dry spell it would take a lot to cause\nissues unless we get hit with strong to severe storms which \nwill likely cause heavy downpours. If areas were to get \nrepeatedly hit and exceed flash flood guidance then there could\nbe issues with flood prone areas. We will have to monitor the \nsituation to get a better grasp on the threats, if any, from \nthis main feature for Thursday. \n\nA very subtle short wave may bring diurnal chances for \nshower/storm activity on Friday during the daytime as well as on\nSaturday as another subtle shortwave crosses the region, \nhowever chances remain very low at this time. A weak disturbance\nis then forecast to impede the area on Sunday keeping low \nchances for precipitation and/or thunderstorm activity on the \ntable through Sunday. Temperatures will rebound during this time\nframe for the weekend to about the climatological norm and \nsouthwesterly flow will pump moisture back into the region \nallowing dewpoints getting back to the 70 degree mark or \nslightly above for the weekend bringing back summertime\nconditions.",
